{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>וַיִּקְרָא, etc.</b> The reason for the calling — and speech alone did not suffice — they said in Torat Kohanim: in three places the calling preceded the speech: at the bush, at Sinai, and at the Tent of Meeting. And we do not find [a basis] to derive the Tent of Meeting either from the bush or from Sinai. From the bush, there is a superiority in that it was the first of all prophetic communications; Sinai has a superiority in that it was for all Israel — and one cannot derive from them [the rule for] the Tent of Meeting. Moreover, one cannot derive the Tent of Meeting from the common element shared by the bush and Sinai, for one can say: what [about] the common element — that they were in fire? — which is not so of the Tent of Meeting." "<b>Here,</b> what they said — that a calling was stated at Sinai — the Rav, Rabbi Eliyahu Mizrachi, wrote that this is the verse ״וַיִּקְרָא אֶל מֹשֶׁה בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי״ etc. (Exodus 24:16), or ״וַיִּקְרָא ה׳ לְמֹשֶׁה אֶל רֹאשׁ הָהָר״ (Exodus 19:20), but not from the verse ״וַיִּקְרָא אֵלָיו ה׳ מִן הָהָר״ (Exodus 19:3), as Rabbenu Hillel explained, for this calling was not said with fire, and the baraita teaches: what about the common element — that it was in fire, etc. — end quote. And with apologies for his honor, he did not examine this precisely, for the truth is that the derivation for [calling preceding] speech is from the verse ״וַיִּקְרָא אֵלָיו ה׳ מִן הָהָר״, for [regarding] ״וַיִּקְרָא אֶל מֹשֶׁה בַּיּוֹם הַזֶּה״ — that calling is not superfluous and it was needed for its own sake, for He called him to ascend to remain on the mountain forty days, as stated there: ״וַיִּקְרָא אֶל מֹשֶׁה״ etc. ״וַיָּבֹא מֹשֶׁה״ etc. ״וַיְהִי מֹשֶׁה בָּהָר אַרְבָּעִים יוֹם״ — and this calling is in place of speech. And the calling that we are discussing is a calling that is for the sake of speech, [where Scripture] mentions both a calling and a speech. Also, ״וַיִּקְרָא ה׳ לְמֹשֶׁה אֶל רֹאשׁ הָהָר״ — this calling was for the purpose of ascent, for Moses was below and He called him to ascend to the top of the mountain, and there He spoke to him; and had He not said ״וַיִּקְרָא״ but had spoken with him, He would have been speaking with him while he was below the mountain. And we see that He did not speak to him until after he ascended — so how could there be mouth and tongue to say that He should have said only ״וַיְדַבֵּר״? But regarding the verse ״וַיִּקְרָא אֵלָיו ה׳ מִן הָהָר״, we see that the purpose of the calling was nothing but to say to him ״כֹּה תֹאמַר״ etc., and these words He said to him there while he was below without ascending. If so, what is the nature of this calling? It is nothing but a prelude to speech." "And what the Rav supported from the verse ״וַיִּקְרָא אֶל מֹשֶׁה״ (Exodus 24:16) — from what the verse says after it: ״וַיִּקְרָא וַיְדַבֵּר״ etc., ״וְיִקְחוּ לִי״ etc. — they did not illuminate before the face of the Rav [that there are] two verses containing two matters that intervene between the calling and the speech: the first, ״וּמַרְאֵה כְּבוֹד ה׳״ etc., and the second, ״וַיָּבֹא מֹשֶׁה״ etc. ״וַיְהִי שָׁם בָּהָר אַרְבָּעִים יוֹם״ etc. And after this the verse says ״וַיְדַבֵּר ה׳״ — at the bush — to alert you and to vindicate what we have written. And who can say that this speech did not take place after his having stayed on the mountain twenty days or thirty days?" "And what he rejected the view of Rabbenu Hillel — who cited the verse ״וַיִּקְרָא אֵלָיו ה׳ מִן הָהָר״ (Exodus 19:3) — on the grounds that there was not yet fire, and they said there in Torat Kohanim: what about the common element of the bush and Sinai — that they were in fire, etc. — the Rav having supposed that the presence of fire at the time of the calling poses the difficulty — this is not so. For the intent of the Tanna is that in that same prophecy this [element of fire] was present as an addition. Since it happened that it had this distinction — that it was in fire, which the Tent of Meeting did not have — it also had the calling preceding [speech]. And according to this, even if there was not yet fire, what of it? Behold, in that same prophecy there was fire. And this prophecy and the prophecy of the bush differ from the prophecy of the Tent of Meeting in that there was no fire in it — and with this, the Rav's difficulty is not difficult. And I wonder how they did not illuminate before his eyes what we have asked." "<b>Further,</b> if it were as he says, how could the Tanna say: what about the common element among them — that they were in fire? For was not ״וַיִּקְרָא אֵלָיו ה׳ מִן הָהָר לֵאמֹר״ (Exodus 19:3) etc. not in fire? And [yet he would] derive from that calling and the calling of the bush from the common element between them — that they were nothing but speech from the holy mouth to Moses, with the calling preceding the speech. And to deepen this difficulty, one will find it thoroughly refuted." "<b>And furthermore</b>, why was it necessary to cite three verses at Sinai in which a calling preceded the speech? For even if we do not count the verse ״וַיִּקְרָא אֵלָיו ה׳ מִן הָהָר״, there are at any rate two [verses] in which a calling preceded the speech according to the Rav's reasoning. And is not the entire effort of the Tanna expended on the extra ״וַיִּקְרָא״ stated in [connection with] the Tent of Meeting, which would not have needed to be stated? And the difficulty grows with its being mentioned twice at Sinai. Rather, it is certain that the Rav's words here were not stated with precision, and according to our view there is no place for any of these difficulties and the preceding ones. From this point onward we find ourselves saying that the principal source for the learning that a calling preceded the speech at Sinai is the verse ״וַיִּקְרָא אֵלָיו ה׳ מִן הָהָר וְגוֹ׳ כֹּה תֹאמַר״, and that is the principal one." "<b>And as for</b> what they said in the baraita: ״Not if you said at Sinai, for that was to all Israel״ and so forth — on its face this is a astonishing thing, for behold there is no commandment that is not to all Israel. And Ba'al Korban Aharon explained ״to all Israel״, and on this side there would have been a need for the voice to be at a slightly diminished level from what He was accustomed to speak with Moses, and therefore it says ״וַיִּקְרָא אֶל מֹשֶׁה״ to inform that He spoke to him in the customary voice and it was not diminished from its level for Israel's sake. And according to his approach one must say as though the Tanna said: on account of its being to all Israel — for that is not a distinction, its being to all Israel, and these words are not acceptable to the intellect. And the correct view in my eyes is that the distinction is that we find that HaShem changed His utterances there, in that He did not say them to Moses alone for him to speak to the children of Israel, but rather from the mouth of the Most High they came to all Israel. And just as the prophecy of Sinai was altered for this particular [distinction], it was likewise altered for this distinction of the calling as compared with the rest of the utterances spoken to Moses alone, which HaShem did not make audible to all Israel — so too it is stated that they did not possess this distinction, that a calling preceded the speech." "<b>Furthermore</b>, it concluded in that same baraita: Perhaps there was a calling only for this utterance? From where [do we learn it] for all the utterances in the Torah? Scripture says: ״מֵאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד״. Perhaps there was a calling only for utterances (dibbur) alone, but from where to include sayings (amirah) and commandments (tzivuy)? Scripture says: ״דַּבֵּר״ ״וַיְדַבֵּר״ — to include even sayings and commandments, and so forth; thus far. The meaning of these words is that from the callings of the Tent of Meeting and the Bush and Sinai one cannot derive [the rule] for any utterance other than them, the reason being because that was the beginning of speech in that place; but whatever else He would speak there afterward in that place would not be preceded by a calling. Scripture says: ״מֵאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד״ — an extra calling, to say that whatever He speaks from the Tent of Meeting is preceded by a calling. And even though the verse includes only the Tent of Meeting, this is a paradigm for all, for there is nothing novel about Sinai nor about the Bush to say that it was on account of that novelty that the calling took place. And it returned to say: I have only utterances (dibbur), for so does the verse strictly imply ״וַיְדַבֵּר״; from where [to derive it] for prophecies that begin with ״וַיֹּאמֶר״ or the language of commandment? For perhaps since dibbur expresses the power of the Speaker in a way that sayings and commandments do not, from where that a calling should precede them? Scripture says: ״דַּבֵּר״ and ״וַיְדַבֵּר״." "And I saw a version of the Ra'avad: ״Scripture says: דַּבֵּר and וַיְדַבֵּר לֵאמֹר״. And according to this version he expounds the extra word ״לֵאמֹר״ stated after ״וַיְדַבֵּר״, which should not have needed to be said after he already said ״דַּבֵּר״ — from this you learn that it comes to include the sayings (amirah), and the commandments are in the category of sayings. And I saw that Ba'al Korban Aharon objected to this version, [asking] why it says ״Scripture says: דַּבֵּר״ when it should have said only ״Scripture says: לֵאמֹר״; thus far. And this is no difficulty, for the force of the derashah derives from the doubling of ״דַּבֵּר״, since without it the word ״לֵאמֹר״ is not extra and is needed for its own sake; therefore the Ra'avad's version is primary." "<b>Now behold,</b> what the Tanna discussed to demonstrate that it is necessary to state ״וַיִּקְרָא״ for the first utterance in the Tent of Meeting — because it is derived neither from the Bush nor from Sinai — this is nothing but the discernment of the truth on its own merits. For even without this, even if the first utterance stated in the Tent of Meeting were to be derived from the Bush and Sinai, there would still be no superfluity in the word ״וַיִּקְרָא״, for it was needed for the rest of the utterances alluded to in the extra word ״מֵאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד״ and those derived from the extra word ״לֵאמֹר״ — for without the mention of the calling here, we would not know the things that are thereby included." "<b>Rather,</b> one must pay attention to the baraita: why did it not raise the issue of the superfluous ״וַיִּקְרָא״ stated at the Burning Bush and at Sinai, so that they would be inferred from the ״וַיִּקְרָא״ stated at the Tent of Meeting? And it is unnecessary to say if one were to say [the comparison involves] Tent of Meeting and Sinai, or Tent of Meeting and the Bush, and the third would come from the common element between them. Furthermore, it is difficult according to the words of the Tanna who expounds the saying of ״מֵאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד״ as extra to expound — to include all the divine speeches that were stated at the Tent of Meeting, which were preceded by a calling, and not the first one alone. If so, what detracts from the first speech that was stated at Sinai, which was not preceded by a calling, such that it was necessary to state about it ״וַיִּקְרָא״? And likewise at the Bush, it should not have been necessary to state ״וַיִּקְרָא,״ according to what he learned there from the superfluous word of the speech ״לֵאמֹר,״ to include all the divine sayings." "<b>And I saw</b> that the Rav, Rabbi Elijah Mizrachi, set his mind to resolving these difficulties, but everything the Rav wrote on this matter does not accord with my understanding. The first thing: what he wrote, that one cannot infer Sinai and the Bush from the Tent of Meeting, because the calling was for the reason that Moses was afraid to enter — therefore He called him to come in. This is difficult, for when we say that the Tanna of Torat Kohanim is concerned with this exposition, it is still possible that no calling preceded the speech at the Tent of Meeting, and this calling was only so that he would enter and not stand outside because he was afraid — and specifically at Sinai and the Bush, because of the common element between them that is not [found] at the Tent of Meeting, is it [namely] that the calling preceded the speech." "<b>And</b> what he resolved — that in truth the view of the Tanna is that we infer the Bush from Sinai and the Tent of Meeting, as he revealed his opinion in expounding (Exodus 3:4) ״וַיֹּאמֶר מֹשֶׁה מֹשֶׁה״ — that there was no need for the verse to say ״וַיֹּאמֶר״ except to teach about all the callings, that they were [in the form of] ״מֹשֶׁה מֹשֶׁה,״ and if he did not hold that there is no need to teach through it that the calling preceded [the speech], since one infers from Sinai and the Tent of Meeting — behold, it would be needed for its own sake, to inform us that the calling preceded it in any event. I am embarrassed by these words, for what we learn — that the calling preceded the speech — does not apply to the speech that was within the calling itself, but rather to the speech stated adjacent to it. For the speech that was within the calling itself — we do not find it in any of the three callings except at the Bush, where the verse explained the manner of the calling, that it was ״מֹשֶׁה מֹשֶׁה,״ and it is not regarding this speech that we say a calling preceded the speech, but regarding the speech stated to him after the calling. And behold, before you are the places about which we say that the calling preceded [the speech]: at Sinai, nothing is stated but ״וַיִּקְרָא,״ and the speech that was within the calling itself is not stated, yet we say about it that the calling preceded the speech — and perforce it refers to the speech stated adjacent to the calling. And likewise at the Tent of Meeting we say that the calling preceded the speech, even though ״וַיֹּאמֶר מֹשֶׁ״ was not stated. If so, the calling at the Bush as well — even if the verse had not said ״וַיֹּאמֶר״ but rather ״וַיִּקְרָא מֹשֶׁה מֹשֶׁה וְגוֹ׳ וַיֹּאמֶר שַׁל נְעָלֶיךָ״ וְגוֹ׳ — we would say that a calling preceded the speech, and the ״וַיֹּאמֶר״ remains superfluous to expound what the Tanna expounded. And I marvel at him, that he explained the calling that preceded the speech at Sinai as the verse ״וַיִּקְרָא בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי,״ and the speech after it as ״וְיִקְחוּ לִי תְּרוּמָה,״ between which there is a great distance, and in the calling at the Bush he averted his eyes from seeing the speeches stated in proximity. And if the Rav thought that the calling is one thing and the saying of ״מֹשֶׁה מֹשֶׁה״ another, this is an error, and the matter is clear to the eye of every understanding person. And I marvel how such words issued from a holy mouth. And there is still more to object to, except that there is no need to reject such things." "<b>And</b> what he resolved — why not infer Sinai and the Bush from all the speeches and sayings that are multiplied in this verse, where the calling preceded them — and he said that the intent of the Tanna was only for those speeches and sayings that were stated at the Tent of Meeting alone, and one may not infer from it to other speeches stated in other places, according to what he raised and which we refuted nearby: for the Bush has no precedent of calling, and the Tent of Meeting [only] because the cloud rested upon it and he could not enter. And he forced himself to explain even in the words of Rashi, who said unqualifiedly that to all the speeches and sayings etc. a calling preceded etc. — that this means only those stated at the Tent of Meeting, end of quote. His words, of blessed memory, are not matched by reason. For from the fact that the verse multiplied all the speeches and sayings that were at the Tent of Meeting, in which the calling preceded — the eye sees and the heart hears that it was not because the cloud rested and he could not enter that the calling existed, but rather to precede the calling to the speech. If the verse were informing [us] that on account of fear a single calling would suffice for all, and if to inform that in every speech when HaShem spoke, Moses was afraid to enter and needed to be called — these words are not fit to be said, for perforce one must say that the matter was not so constantly, for the priests enter and minister, and Moses was not afraid or terrified to enter with them. And from the fact that HaShem spoke with him constantly — unless you say that every time HaShem wished to speak with him, the cloud would rest and the priests would go out outside the Tent of Meeting, and this is complete nonsense." "<b>Further,</b> you will find that the Re'em raised, in his comments on ״מֵאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד,״ that the Tanna of Torat Kohanim holds like that baraita which teaches (beginning of Torat Kohanim): One verse says (Exodus 40:35) ״וְלֹא יָכֹל מֹשֶׁה לָבֹא,״ and one verse says (Numbers 7:89) ״וּבְבֹא מֹשֶׁה.״ The third verse came and decided between them (Exodus 40:35) ״כִּי שָׁכַן עָלָיו הֶעָנָן.״ Say from now on: all the time the cloud was on the Tabernacle he could not; once it withdrew etc. ״וּבְבֹא״ וְגוֹ׳, end of quote. Now, the simple sense of this exposition is that Moses never entered except after the cloud withdrew, and even so the verse multiplied for all the speeches that were stated to him at the Tent of Meeting, that the calling preceded them. And if you say that the Tanna did not expound thus, but only after a calling was stated at Sinai — but besides this, he would have been searching for the exposition of Rabbi Tanhuma (beginning of this portion) who expounded that the calling was because he was afraid to enter — behold you have the exposition of Rabbi Tanhuma, of blessed memory: Moses was afraid to enter, as it is written ״וְלֹא יָכֹל מֹשֶׁה.״ The Holy One, blessed be He, said: it is only right and proper that Moses, who labored for the Tabernacle, should stand outside while I am inside. Rather, I will call him to enter; therefore it says ״וַיִּקְרָא.״ According to this reasoning, the [special] calling was only the first time, but from then on permission was given to him from the King to enter, and there was no fear. And even so, the verse says that a calling preceded the speech. From now on, why do we not learn all the speeches — and it is unnecessary to say Sinai and the Bush — from the Tent of Meeting?" "<b>And the correct interpretation</b> to say is this: had the verse not stated the precedence of calling before speaking at Sinai and at the bush, even though it says 'in the Tent of Meeting,' we would have said that 'in the Tent of Meeting' restricts Sinai and the bush, where calling did not precede — for reason would lead us to learn from the Tent of Meeting: just as calling preceded speaking there, so too at the bush and at Sinai. Therefore the verse says ״מֵאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד״. And there is a reason for this restriction, for perhaps, since HaShem established His Shekhinah with Israel, He adds this quality at the inception of His Shekhinah — that He called to him before speaking — and the inclusion of ״לֵאמֹר״ we would then expound in another manner. And you should know that there is no parashah that does not contain an extra ״לֵאמֹר״ and yet we expound it in a different way, and this would be one of those. And after the verse stated calling at the bush and at Sinai, we are obligated to say that 'from the Tent of Meeting' comes to include the other utterances; and after we find that the verse included the utterances, we also expound ״לֵאמֹר״ for what stands before us — namely, the utterances (amirót) about which I am undecided whether they fall under the category of the dibrot (utterances), for we have far more reason to expound the extra word for something we need in its place than to seek out other derashot. And after the verse informed us of all the utterances and amirót that were said in the Tent of Meeting, that calling preceded them — the same judgment applies to all the utterances and amirót stated in the Torah. And the reason is that in three places we find that the verse preceded calling to amirah: Sinai, the bush, and the Tent of Meeting. And I do not know whether this matter occurred only the first time or each and every time. When the verse explicates regarding one of them — namely, the Tent of Meeting — that it applied to every utterance and amirah, behold, the hidden thing is revealed: the same judgment holds for all the utterances that occurred in all the places. Or we may say in this manner: after the verse explicated utterances and amirót that occurred in the Tent of Meeting, we turn and learn from Sinai and the bush and the Tent of Meeting to all utterances in this way: just as at Sinai, etc., calling preceded — so too all, etc.; just as Sinai and the bush were the inception of speech in that place, would you say the same of other utterances that were not the inception of speech in that place? The Tent of Meeting proves that calling preceded each and every utterance that occurred there. And when we say: just as the Tent of Meeting was a place where HaShem dwelt permanently and the Divine became one who dwells among us — Sinai and the bush prove, etc., that calling preceded the first time, etc., and the judgment recurs, etc. — the common factor among them is that there was speech from the Mouth of the Holy One to Moses, so too I will bring every speech from the Mouth of the Holy One to Moses, that calling preceded it in every place where He spoke with him." "<b>And He called to Moses.</b> It is necessary to know why the name of the Caller is not mentioned, for even though it is mentioned afterward, it would be fitting to mention it at the outset — and then it would be self-understood that He is the One speaking, when it says ״וַיִּקְרָא ה׳ וְגוֹ׳ וַיְדַבֵּר״." "Or perhaps the verse intends to inform us of His overwhelming might, may He be blessed, that He calls in a great voice and none hears it other than whom He desires — and this is its saying <b>and He called to Moses</b>, that even though He called, the speech was heard only by Moses and not by those who stood before him. According to this, if the verse had said ״וַיִּקְרָא ה׳ אֶל מֹשֶׁה״, you would understand that HaShem called in a loud voice, but the arrival of the voice to Moses was in a low voice — so that Moses would reckon in his mind that it was possible that those who were somewhat distant and were not close to him would not hear, and there would be no novel wonder of HaShem here. Therefore it says <b>and He called to Moses</b> — meaning that even for Moses himself the calling was in a great voice, that he heard a great voice, and these are indeed His awesome wonders, may He be blessed, that none but he heard." "Further, a reason appears why His name, may He be blessed, is not mentioned in the calling: that it is more to the credit of the Name to mention it in a speech of commandment than in the calling, which is nothing but a mere summons or a mark of honor according to the midrash. Further, one may give a reason according to the midrash (Leviticus Rabbah 1:8), where they said that Aaron and his sons and the elders would say: ״We do not know which of them is more beloved before the Place, but rather whomever HaShem calls to״ — and so ״וַיִּקְרָא אֶל מֹשֶׁה״, etc. Now according to this there is no place to mention His name in the calling, for the statement ״וַיִּקְרָא״ is the matter of discernment that the great ones of Israel were awaiting — to whom HaShem would call. Therefore it should say nothing other than ״וַיִּקְרָא אֶל מֹשֶׁה״ — and understand this." "And according to this approach, what remains to be examined further is resolved: why was it necessary to say <b>to Moses</b>, and why was it not sufficient to say <b>to him</b>, which refers back to Moses, who was mentioned nearby (Exodus 40:35)? And similar to this you will find that the verse says at the beginning of the parashah of Vayeira ״אֵלָיו״ and does not mention to whom He appeared, relying on the memory of Abraham from the preceding parashah. So too it should have said here — but according to what we have written, it is not difficult." "<b>And HaShem spoke to him from the Tent of Meeting.</b> The reason it was necessary to say ״to him״ is so that you should not say that HaShem did not withhold His voice from being heard by someone other than Moses — except in the estimation of Israel; but when He would speak in the Tent of Meeting, He would not withhold the hearing of the utterance from those who happened to be there, who are His ministers, the priests of HaShem. Therefore it said ״to him from the Tent of Meeting״ — that even when Moses would enter the Tent of Meeting and none but the priests of HaShem were present there, the utterance would not be heard by anyone but Moses." "<b>From the Tent of Meeting, etc.</b> It is difficult, for it would be proper to precede the specification of the place from which the utterance came, and afterward mention to whom He would speak; yet here He delayed, saying ״to Moses״ first and then saying ״from the Tent of Meeting.״ But according to what they derived in Torat Kohanim, which I wrote above — for they said that ״from the Tent of Meeting״ comes to inform us concerning all the utterances that were said thereafter from the Tent of Meeting, that the calling preceded them — there is reason in the delaying of this specification, to proclaim from afar, as it were. Understand this." "<b>And in Torat</b> Kohanim they further derived: ״from the Tent of Meeting״ teaches that the sound was cut off and did not go out from the Tent of Meeting — end of quote. And it is difficult: have they not already derived from it the matter of the calling? Further it is difficult: if the sound was cut off and did not go outside, why did the verse need to exclude Israel from hearing the utterance? And I saw that the Rabbi, Rabbi Elijah Mizrachi, wrote that the force of their derivation is from the fact that it did not say ״and He called to Moses from the Tent of Meeting,״ but said ״to him from the Tent of Meeting״ — they derived that it intends to say that from the Tent of Meeting it came to him without interruption; from this you learn that it did not go out. And the second difficulty the Ra'avad answered: that which required the verse to exclude them refers to the utterances that preceded the erection of the Tabernacle." "<b>And my mind</b> is not satisfied with either of these two answers. From what Rabbi Elijah Mizrachi wrote, who derives from the delaying of the mention of ״from the Tent of Meeting״ — surely the change makes no impression that one could infer from it, for even though it is understood that from the Tent of Meeting [the sound] went out to the ear of Moses, nevertheless it is not forced from this to say that the sound did not go out, for perhaps Moses was standing near the Tent of Meeting and it always went outside. Moreover, according to his reasoning he should have preceded the beginning of the sound and afterward its destination, in this manner: ״from the Tent of Meeting to him,״ and from the fact that he preceded ״to him,״ this negates that he intended through this juxtaposition to say so — for how can one precede the measure of the end of the utterance before its beginning? And it appears more correct to say that the derivation is from His saying ״from the Tent of Meeting, saying (leimor),״ and He did not say ״and HaShem spoke from the Tent of Meeting to him, saying (leimor)״ — for it would be proper to precede the place of the speaker and the mention of the one addressed; and all the preceding derivations exist: there is the extra ״from the Tent of Meeting״ to derive the other utterances that the calling preceded; there is also the extraordinary fact that He did not say ״and He called from the Tent of Meeting״; there is also the derivation of ״saying (leimor)״ to include other statements and commandments that the calling preceded — hear from this that He intended to say in this manner: ״from the Tent of Meeting, saying (leimor)״ — meaning, from it is the beginning of the utterance alluded to in His saying ״and He spoke,״ and in it the statement culminated in His saying ״saying (leimor)״; from this you learn that it did not go outside. Or we may say that this Tanna does not derive the extra ״Tent of Meeting״ to include the other utterances, but derives both the other utterances and all the statements from the extra word ״saying (leimor)״ — and since it is superfluous, if its subject is not the speaker, assign it as a subject of the listener, that it should not be heard except by those standing in the Tent of Meeting and not by those standing outside. And perhaps He said ״from the Tent״ to exclude even those who were in the Tent of Meeting, namely Aaron and his sons; for although they were excluded by the word ״to him,״ it is still possible to err and say that His saying ״to him״ excluded the elders, but Aaron and his sons have grounds to say they were not excluded; therefore He said ״from the Tent of Meeting.״" "<b>And that</b> which the Ra'avad answered for the second difficulty — that the exclusion applies to the utterances that preceded — is astonishing, for behold the exclusion is in His calling from the Tent of Meeting, and also according to the reasoning of that Tanna who excludes them from the verse (Exodus 25:22) ״I will speak with you״ etc. and (Numbers 7:89) ״the voice to him״ — all of this was in the Tent of Meeting. And it is more correct to say that they did not derive that the sound was cut off in the Tent of Meeting, but rather in what He spoke to him in the Tent of Meeting, as it is written ״and He spoke to him from the Tent of Meeting״; but regarding the calling, he concedes that the sound went outside to call Moses, who was standing outside." "<b>And what</b> the master Rabbi Eliyahu Mizrahi wrote, that this Tanna holds that Moses was inside, and that it was at the time when the cloud withdrew, and the opposing midrash — for it is written ״וְלֹא יָכֹל מֹשֶׁה״ etc. (Exodus 40:35), and it is written ״וּבְבֹא מֹשֶׁה״ (Numbers 7:89) — a third verse comes and decides between them: ״כִּי שָׁכַן הֶעָנָן״ etc.; from now on, all the time that the cloud was present etc. — thus far. And I say that the author of this exposition would not deny that the time of which ״וַיִּקְרָא אֶל מֹשֶׁה״ was spoken was at the time when the cloud dwelt, and the juxtaposition of verses proceeds in this manner: it is written ״וּבְבֹא מֹשֶׁה״ — implying that permission was in his hand at all times; and it is written ״וְלֹא יָכֹל״ etc. — meaning, only by means of a calling. And so you will find that the verse says at the end of the judgments section ״וַיִּקְרָא אֶל מֹשֶׁה וַיָּבֹא מֹשֶׁה בְּתוֹךְ הֶעָנָן״ (Exodus 24:18), and likewise in our own passage. And the matter is straightforward to me, for there is no one who would suppose that Moses lingered outside the Tent of Meeting until after the cloud withdrew and He called him. And you will find that the beginning of that baraita teaches as follows, and this is its language: ״I might exclude all of them, but not the ministering angels, since Moses cannot enter within them until he is called״ — the verse states: ״קוֹל לוֹ״ etc. ״מֵאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד״ — this teaches that the voice would cease etc. — thus far. Behold that the Tanna himself holds that he would not enter except after a calling — you have thus learned that he was outside the Tent of Meeting. And if you say: if so, from where does the Tanna derive the exposition that the calling preceded the speaking — was not this calling only for the purpose of allowing him to enter because he was afraid? It appears that if there had been no need for the calling to precede the speaking as stated, HaShem could have removed the dread from his heart and he would have entered without a calling. Rather, because the calling preceded the speaking — that is why He called as mentioned, and Moses was afraid to enter the Tent of Meeting to present himself so that he would be ready when called, and he remained standing outside until HaShem called him as was His practice, and then he entered. And the voice of the calling went forth to the outside, while the voice of the speaking remained in its place in the Tent of Meeting and did not go forth to be heard — even its enunciation — outside the tent." "<b>And by way of</b> remez (allusion), it alludes that Moses would say to Israel there in the Tent of Meeting all that HaShem would command him; therefore the verse juxtaposes <b>מֵאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד לֵאמֹר</b> — meaning, from there he would speak to Israel. And there is a good reason for this: so that the awe of the spoken word would be upon its hearers before the commanding King, blessed be He, and also so that they would vindicate him all the more when he would say the matters before Him, blessed be He — that he neither left out nor added to His words." "Further, it alludes in this manner to <b>אֵלָיו מֵאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד</b> — meaning that Moses did not attain to have HaShem speak to him except inasmuch as HaShem pardoned the sin of the calf and dwelt in the Tent of Meeting as a sign and a wonder; but had there not been the forgiveness for Israel alluded to in אֹהֶל מוֹעֵד, HaShem would not have spoken to him. And He told him to say this matter to Israel, which is His saying <b>לֵאמֹר</b>. And you will find that they said there in Torat Kohanim: ״לֵאמֹר״ — say to them words of appeasement: on your account He speaks with me etc. — thus far. And that corresponds to our interpretation, except that their exposition derives entirely from the word ״לֵאמֹר״ and not from the precise wording we cited. And perhaps this too is what our Rabbis of blessed memory intended. Further, our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Torat Kohanni, here): ״I might think He spoke with him for His own need — the verse states לֵאמֹר: for Israel's sake He spoke with him and not for His own sake״ — thus far. The intention of their words is that the word ״לֵאמֹר״ is a restrictive term, to restrict that His speaking to Moses was not for anything other than for Israel's sake — meaning, to speak to them. From here you learn that HaShem did not speak with Moses except words of Torah that he transmitted to Israel, and it is the statement of the Tanna (Mishnah Avot 1:1): Moses received Torah from Sinai and handed it on to Joshua — you have thus learned that everything he received he transmitted. And from here you learn that HaShem did not inform him of a secret that he did not teach Israel, and Israel were equated to Moses in knowledge, except that he learned from the mouth of the Almighty and Israel from the mouth of a man."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :register {:flags {27 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {15 "The author uses דִּבּוּרִים and אֲמִירוֹת as two distinct categories of divine utterance; I have rendered the former as 'utterances' and transliterated the latter as 'amirót' to preserve the distinction he is debating. The frequent וְכוּ׳ marks in the latter half indicate truncated midrashic-formula citations; I have rendered them literally as 'etc.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:55:04.983035312Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}